# Target C-II — near-disk locality removes shallow-tooth multiplicity

**Date:** 2026-09-14  
**Repository branch:** `research/c2-effective-sixpair-sol-20260914`  
**Target:** Target C-II, full-core near-disk branch  
**Claim class:** proof-only geometric strengthening of the shallow-tooth penetration theorem  
**Dependencies:** canonical `C-FULLCORE-NEAR-DISK`; outer-tooth exactness; C26 contact-degree bounds; triangle-freeness  
**LEVEL I STATUS:** `STILL OPEN`

The existing shallow-tooth theorem loses two factors of two.  It allows one unloaded
apex to support two shallow teeth, and then allows one selected penetration edge to be
charged from both endpoints.  In the canonical Type-II near-disk branch neither loss
is real.

For every fixed

\[
0<\eta<2-\sqrt3,
\tag{NL.1}
\]

and all sufficiently large Type-II survivors:

1. one unloaded apex supports at most one `eta`-shallow two-edge tooth;
2. two `eta`-shallow tooth apices are never joined by a unit contact; and therefore
3. one may choose a **distinct** inward penetration contact for every shallow tooth.

Consequently, if `N_sh(eta)` is the shallow-tooth count and `E_pen(eta)` is the number
of distinct selected penetration contacts, then the previous factor-four estimate

\[
E_{pen}(\eta)\ge {N_{sh}(\eta)\over4}
\]

improves to

\[
\boxed{
E_{pen}(\eta)\ge N_{sh}(\eta)
\ge N_2-{d_{out}\over\eta}.
}
\tag{NL.2}
\]

Every selected edge still has a fixed positive inward normal component.

---

## 1. Geometry of one shallow tooth

Let `a-x-b` be a two-edge tooth.  The loaded endpoints `a,b` are consecutive vertices
of the convex force hull `F`, while the unloaded apex `x` lies in `F`.  Put

\[
s=|a-b|\ge2-\eta.
\]

Write

\[
s=2\cos\alpha,
\qquad
0\le\alpha\le\alpha_\eta:=\arccos(1-\eta/2).
\tag{NL.3}
\]

Let `n` be the outward unit normal to the hull side `ab`, and let `t` be a unit tangent.
Because `xa=xb=1`, the vectors from the apex to the two base endpoints are

\[
\boxed{
a-x=\sin\alpha\,n+\cos\alpha\,t,
\qquad
b-x=\sin\alpha\,n-\cos\alpha\,t,
}
\tag{NL.4}
\]

up to exchanging `a,b` and the sign of `t`.  Thus the apex lies only

\[
h=\sin\alpha\le h_\eta:=\sin\alpha_\eta
\tag{NL.5}
\]

inside the supporting line of `F` containing `ab`.

Condition (NL.1) is exactly

\[
\boxed{\alpha_\eta<\pi/6.}
\tag{NL.6}
\]

---

## 2. Near-disk support normals are local at tooth apices

Canonical `C-FULLCORE-NEAR-DISK` gives, after translating the force hull,

\[
B(0,r_D)\subset F\subset B(0,R_D),
\qquad
{r_D\over D},{R_D\over D}\to{1\over2}.
\tag{NL.7}
\]

For a tooth with outward base normal `n`, the support value satisfies

\[
h_F(n)\ge r_D.
\]

Since the apex is at inward distance at most `h_eta` from that support line,

\[
n\cdot x\ge r_D-h_\eta.
\tag{NL.8}
\]

Also `|x|<=R_D`.  Therefore, writing `e_x=x/|x|`,

\[
\boxed{
n\cdot e_x
\ge {r_D-h_\eta\over R_D}=1-o(1).
}
\tag{NL.9}
\]

The estimate is uniform over all `eta`-shallow teeth because `eta` is fixed.  Hence

\[
\boxed{
\angle(n,e_x)=o(1).
}
\tag{NL.10}
\]

This is the key locality input.  A fixed-length shallow tooth on a diameter-`D`
near-disk body has its base normal asymptotically equal to the radial direction of its
apex.

---

## 3. One apex cannot support two shallow teeth

Suppose one unloaded vertex `x` supports two distinct `eta`-shallow teeth, with base
normals `n_1,n_2` and tangent directions `t_1,t_2`.  Equation (NL.10) gives

\[
\angle(n_1,n_2)=o(1),
\qquad
\angle(t_1,t_2)=o(1).
\tag{NL.11}
\]

For tooth `j`, equation (NL.4) says its two loaded contact directions lie within
`alpha_eta` of `+t_j` and `-t_j`, respectively.  Match the endpoint near `+t_1` with the
endpoint near `+t_2`, and likewise on the negative side.  Each matched pair of contact
directions has angular separation at most

\[
2\alpha_\eta+o(1).
\tag{NL.12}
\]

By (NL.6), for all sufficiently large `D`,

\[
2\alpha_\eta+o(1)<\pi/3.
\tag{NL.13}
\]

But two distinct source points at unit distance from the same point `x` have angular
separation at least `pi/3`, because their mutual distance is at least one.  Therefore
each matched endpoint pair must in fact be the same source point.  The two teeth have
the same loaded endpoint pair and hence the same hull side, contradicting distinctness.

Thus

\[
\boxed{
\text{every unloaded vertex is the apex of at most one `eta`-shallow tooth.}
}
\tag{NL.14}
\]

This improves the purely local degree/matching bound of two teeth per apex.

---

## 4. Strong inwardness holds throughout `eta<2-sqrt3`

The previous shallow-tooth note proved the inward-contact estimate only under the
smaller auxiliary threshold `alpha_eta<pi/12`, because that threshold was also being
used there for the two-teeth-per-apex matching argument.  The inward estimate itself
needs only (NL.6).

Choose coordinates with the base side on `y=0`, the force hull in `y>=0`, and

\[
a=(-\cos\alpha,0),
\quad
b=(\cos\alpha,0),
\quad
x=(0,\sin\alpha).
\]

Let `w` be any contact neighbor of `x` distinct from `a,b`, and write

\[
w-x=(X,Y),
\qquad X^2+Y^2=1.
\]

Triangle-freeness gives `|w-a|>1` and `|w-b|>1`.  Expanding yields

\[
\cos\alpha\,|X|-\sin\alpha\,Y<1/2.
\tag{NL.15}
\]

Write

\[
Y=\sin\beta,
\qquad |X|=\cos\beta,
\qquad \beta\in[-\pi/2,\pi/2].
\]

Since `w in F subset {y>=0}`,

\[
\beta\ge-\alpha.
\tag{NL.16}
\]

Equation (NL.15) becomes

\[
\cos(\alpha+\beta)<1/2.
\tag{NL.17}
\]

By (NL.16), `alpha+beta>=0`; by (NL.6) and `beta<=pi/2`,

\[
\alpha+\beta<2\pi/3.
\]

Hence (NL.17) forces

\[
\beta>\pi/3-\alpha.
\]

Therefore every extra contact has inward component

\[
\boxed{
Y>Y_\eta
:=\sin(\pi/3-\alpha_\eta)>0.
}
\tag{NL.18}
\]

Equivalently,

\[
\boxed{
Y_\eta
={\sqrt3\over2}(1-\eta/2)
-{1\over2}\sqrt{\eta-\eta^2/4}.
}
\tag{NL.19}
\]

---

## 5. Two shallow apices cannot be contact-adjacent

Suppose distinct `eta`-shallow apices `x,y` satisfy

\[
|x-y|=1.
\tag{NL.20}
\]

Let `n_x,n_y` be the outward normals of their respective tooth bases and put

\[
m_x=-n_x,
\qquad m_y=-n_y
\]

for the inward normals.

Equation (NL.8) gives

\[
|x|,|y|\ge r_D-h_\eta.
\tag{NL.21}
\]

The law of cosines and (NL.20) then show that the polar angle between `x` and `y` is
`O(D^{-1})`.  Combining this with (NL.10),

\[
\boxed{
\|m_x-m_y\|=o(1).
}
\tag{NL.22}
\]

Because tooth apices are unloaded while tooth base endpoints are loaded, `y` is an
extra contact neighbor of `x`, and `x` is an extra contact neighbor of `y`.  Apply
(NL.18) in both directions.  With

\[
u=y-x,
\qquad |u|=1,
\]

we obtain

\[
u\cdot m_x>Y_\eta,
\qquad
(-u)\cdot m_y>Y_\eta.
\tag{NL.23}
\]

Adding,

\[
2Y_\eta
< u\cdot(m_x-m_y)
\le\|m_x-m_y\|=o(1),
\]

contradicting the fixed positivity of `Y_eta`.

Therefore

\[
\boxed{
\text{the set of `eta`-shallow tooth apices is independent in the contact graph.}
}
\tag{NL.24}
\]

---

## 6. One distinct penetration edge per shallow tooth

Canonical C26 gives every unloaded contact-core vertex degree at least three.  Thus each
shallow tooth apex `x` has some contact neighbor `w` distinct from its two loaded base
endpoints.

By (NL.14), different shallow teeth have different apices.  By (NL.24), no selected
extra contact can join two shallow apices.  Hence an edge selected from one shallow
apex cannot be selected from any other shallow apex.  The selected edges are therefore
all distinct.

Together with the exact outer-detour Markov bound,

\[
N_{sh}(\eta)
\ge N_2-{d_{out}\over\eta},
\]

we obtain

\[
\boxed{
E_{pen}(\eta)
\ge N_{sh}(\eta)
\ge N_2-{d_{out}\over\eta}.
}
\tag{NL.25}
\]

Every selected edge satisfies the local inward projection floor (NL.18).

This is an unweighted physical-edge theorem.  No KKT coefficient lower bound is used.

---

## 7. Numerical consequence at the `g=0.00335` endpoint

Combine this theorem with the proof-only `eta_grid=0.00335` propagation on the present
branch.  The tooth alternative gives

\[
{N_2\over D}
\ge0.436291371870117\ldots-o(1),
\tag{NL.26}
\]

and Section 171 gives

\[
{d_{out}\over D}
\le0.002803625+o(1).
\tag{NL.27}
\]

Choose the convenient fixed threshold

\[
\eta=0.06<2-\sqrt3.
\]

Then

\[
\boxed{
{N_{sh}(0.06)\over D}
\ge0.389564288536783\ldots-o(1).
}
\tag{NL.28}
\]

By (NL.25), the same lower bound holds for **distinct penetration contacts**:

\[
\boxed{
{E_{pen}(0.06)\over D}
\ge0.389564288536783\ldots-o(1).
}
\tag{NL.29}
\]

For `eta=0.06`,

\[
\boxed{
Y_{0.06}
={97\sqrt3-\sqrt{591}\over200}
=0.718492183859473\ldots .
}
\tag{NL.30}
\]

Thus the sum of the local inward-normal projections over the selected distinct edges
is at least

\[
\boxed{
0.279898896424455\ldots D-o(D).
}
\tag{NL.31}
\]

The normals vary around the hull, so (NL.31) is a sum of local scalar penetrations, not
a single global vector projection.

---

## 8. Frontier effect

The tooth branch is now much more rigid:

```text
low t + tooth alternative
 -> >0.38956 D shallow actual teeth
 -> >0.38956 D distinct unloaded shallow apices
 -> those apices form a contact-independent set
 -> >0.38956 D distinct inward unit contacts
 -> each advances >0.71849 in its own local inward normal
```

This removes both multiplicity losses in the previous shallow-tooth theorem.  A closing
argument can now try to charge a linear independent boundary-apex population and a
linear set of distinct inward contacts directly to

1. the almost pure pentagon closure network,
2. harmonic-cell attachment capacity, or
3. a simultaneous source-preserving shear of many disjoint tooth neighborhoods.

The long-side alternative remains separate.  C-II and Level I are still open.
